From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: d2d1: effect graphs as Paint.NET builds them; DrawImage's image rectangle for effects

Three things Paint.NET 5's canvas and colour controls depend on:

- One effect input may feed several transform nodes. The graph kept one
  node per effect input, so a second ConnectToEffectInput replaced the
  first: Paint.NET's "Convert Alpha" connects its input to a premultiply
  and an un-premultiply node and picks one as the output -- which then had
  no input and drew nothing (the canvas's layer tiles, the colour wheel,
  swatches and palette). Each node now remembers which effect input feeds
  each of its inputs.
- A transform is given its draw info when it is added to a graph. It was
  only given it once, after Initialize, so an effect that rebuilds its graph
  in PrepareForRender (Paint.NET's affine transform) had no pixel shader
  and drew nothing.
- DrawImage with an image rectangle puts that rectangle's top left at the
  target offset for effects, as it already did for bitmaps and command
  lists (Paint.NET's checkerboard was drawn offset by the canvas margin).

Gate: test/d2dgraph-gate.sh (make test-d2dgraph; stock fails 3 of 5).
